---
import StoryLayout from '../layouts/StoryLayout.astro';
import StoryArt from '../components/StoryArt.astro';
import AgentMeta from '../components/AgentMeta.astro';
const siteOrigin = import.meta.env.FROGLET_SITE_ORIGIN || 'https://froglet.dev';
const agentGuide = `${siteOrigin}/publish/agent.md`;
const socialImage = {
  src: 'https://froglet.dev/og/home.png',
  width: 1200,
  height: 630,
  type: 'image/png',
  alt: 'Froglet: share data and tools on your terms. Bob shares a service from his desk and Alice uses it from hers.',
};
---
<StoryLayout title="Froglet — Share data and tools. On your terms." description="Make your data and useful tools available to other people and their AI assistants. Choose what to share, review it, and send a link." path="/" image={socialImage}>
  <AgentMeta slot="head" route="home" />
  <div class="story-wrap">
    <section class="story-hero">
      <div class="story-hero__copy">
        <span class="story-kicker">Useful work deserves to be used.</span>
        <h1>Share data<br />and tools.<br /><span class="story-accent">On your terms.</span></h1>
        <p class="story-lede">Let someone look up information or run a useful task. Choose what they can use, review it with your AI assistant, and share a link.</p>
        <div class="story-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/publish/">Share something useful</a><a class="btn secondary" href="#how-it-works">See how it works</a></div>
        <p class="story-note">Public beta · Start with a free service on your own computer.</p>
        <div class="story-needs" role="group" aria-labelledby="hero-needs-label">
          <span class="story-needs__label" id="hero-needs-label">Today you’ll need</span>
          <ul role="list"><li>Codex or Claude Code</li><li>Apple Silicon macOS or Linux</li><li>A computer that stays online</li></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="story-hero__art story-hero__art--scene"><StoryArt name="01-homepage-hero" width={1600} height={900} eager sizes="(max-width: 760px) 155vw, 850px" alt="Bob shares a service from his desk. Alice uses it from hers." /></div>
    </section>
    <div class="story-strip"><span>Your choice of data</span><span>Your own hosting</span><span>Open source</span><span>For people and their AI assistants</span></div>

    <section class="story-section" id="what-you-can-share">
      <div class="story-heading"><span class="story-kicker">More than sending a file</span><h2>Share something people can use.</h2><p>A file gives someone a copy. A Froglet service lets them ask a question or run a task and get a result. Their AI assistant handles the connection.</p></div>
      <div class="story-grid">
        <article class="story-use"><span class="story-kicker">01 / Data</span><h3>A useful collection</h3><p>A research catalog, a product list, or a table that translates your terminology into someone else’s. Share the fields you choose.</p></article>
        <article class="story-use"><span class="story-kicker">02 / Functions</span><h3>A repeatable task</h3><p>A calculation, a lookup, or a small analysis. Someone provides an input; your tool returns the answer.</p></article>
        <article class="story-use"><span class="story-kicker">03 / Existing tools</span><h3>One useful operation</h3><p>Give access to a specific task your system already performs, with defined inputs and limits. This takes additional setup.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" id="how-it-works">
      <div class="story-heading"><span class="story-kicker">Bob has something useful. Alice needs it.</span><h2>A small service.<br />A simple way to share it.</h2></div>
      <div class="story-grid">
        <article class="story-step"><StoryArt name="02-walkthrough-choose" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 360px" /><span class="story-kicker">01 / Choose</span><h3>Pick what to share.</h3><p>Bob asks his assistant to prepare a useful part of his work. Only the selected data goes into the shared copy.</p></article>
        <article class="story-step"><StoryArt name="03-walkthrough-preview" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 360px" /><span class="story-kicker">02 / Review</span><h3>See it work first.</h3><p>Bob sees what is included, what is left out, and an example result. He approves before anything is published.</p></article>
        <article class="story-step"><StoryArt name="04-walkthrough-publish" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 360px" /><span class="story-kicker">03 / Share</span><h3>Send Alice a link.</h3><p>Alice’s assistant reads what the service does and helps her decide whether to use it. Invitation access, when required, is sent separately.</p></article>
      </div>
      <a href="/publish/">Share something useful</a>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section story-split" id="your-control">
      <div><span class="story-kicker">You decide the scope</span><h2>Share the useful part.<br />Keep the rest yours.</h2><ul class="story-checks">
        <li><strong>Choose the data and the task.</strong><span>Review the exact fields or operation that others will be able to use.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Decide who can use it.</strong><span>Start with public sharing, or set up invitations with expiry and request limits.</span></li>
        <li><strong>Change your mind.</strong><span>Review updates before publishing them. Pause the service or revoke invitations to stop new work.</span></li>
      </ul><p class="story-note">A shared result can be kept by its recipient. Stopping access prevents new work; it cannot recall a result already delivered.</p></div>
      <StoryArt name="06-discovery" alt="Alice examines the service that is relevant to her work." />
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" id="organizations">
      <div class="story-org"><div class="story-split"><div><span class="story-kicker">For organizations</span><h2>Collaboration shouldn’t start with an infrastructure project.</h2></div><div><p class="story-lede">Agree what can be shared, who can use it, and under which conditions.</p><p>Our direction is to turn those decisions into a working service. Today, Froglet provides selected data sharing, invitations, execution limits, and signed records. Broader contract and SLA automation is still ahead.</p><a href="/managed/">See what organizations can control today</a></div></div><StoryArt name="11-distributed-world" width={1600} height={900} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 1080px" alt="Independent workspaces connected directly, without a central platform." /></div>
    </section>

    <section class="story-section" id="questions">
      <div class="story-heading"><span class="story-kicker">A few practical questions</span><h2>Understand it before you start.</h2></div>
      <div class="story-faq">
        <details><summary>Is this like a shared folder?</summary><p>That is a useful starting point. Froglet shares a service: something another person’s assistant can query or run. You host it on a computer you control. It is not a hosted file-storage account, and there is no drag-and-drop web workspace today.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do I need to know how to code?</summary><p>For a small data catalog, your assistant can help prepare it. Today you use a file-capable assistant such as Codex or Claude Code on Apple Silicon macOS or Linux. Installation and publishing still require your review. Connecting an existing system or setting up invitations needs more technical configuration.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is private, and what becomes public?</summary><p>You choose the data included in the service. Public services are accessible to others; invited services need a separate access credential. A service’s description, example, and selected package information may still be public. Keep confidential data and credentials out of that material.</p><p>Invitations currently cover the services on one Froglet host. Use separate hosts when different recipients should have different access. <a href="/learn/share-services/#invite-access-and-bounded-http-tools">Read the access guide</a>.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can I charge for what I share?</summary><p>Paid services are part of the vision, and payment integrations exist with different levels of maturity. The guided publishing journey starts free. It is not yet a ready-to-use sales and billing service. <a href="/open-source/#integrations">See payment integration status</a>.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Can someone run their own program on my computer?</summary><p>Yes, if you choose to offer bounded computation. Alice can supply a compatible WebAssembly program and input; Bob’s Froglet Node runs it with agreed memory, time, and execution limits. The native agent tool supports this, with an optional A2A connection between configured Nodes.</p><p>The repeatable demo uses two local Nodes and free jobs. Public setup and real payments need further qualification. This does not let arbitrary programs use your files or network. <a href="/learn/agent-interoperability/">See the working flow and its limits</a>.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Where does my service run?</summary><p>On your computer or infrastructure. That machine needs to stay awake, online, and running Froglet. Managed always-on hosting is not available yet. This is a public beta; clean-machine and first-time-user qualification are still pending.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does Froglet enforce every part of an agreement?</summary><p>It can apply supported technical controls, such as selected data, invitation access, and execution limits. Signed records make an exchange inspectable. They do not establish that a result is correct or automatically enforce legal terms, data-use promises, or an uptime SLA.</p></details>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="story-closing"><span class="story-kicker">Start small</span><h2>What do you have<br />that someone else could use?</h2><p>One catalog. One lookup. One useful calculation.</p><div class="story-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="/publish/">Share something useful</a><a class="btn secondary" href="/marketplace/">Explore services</a></div><p class="story-note" style="margin-top:24px"><a href={agentGuide}>Instructions for your AI assistant</a> · <a href="/open-source/">For developers</a></p></section>
  </div>
</StoryLayout>
